# SMLSC Club - Project ISA

> Project ISA (system of record). Lives in the project folder per PAI doctrine. Supersedes the `context/{facts,state,references,feedback}.md` foundation pattern: those files fold into the sections below and are retained as detailed appendices until fully absorbed. The `USER/PROJECTS/club-renewal.md` entry in PAI is the lightweight index that points here.

## Problem

South Melbourne Lifesaving Club (SMLSC) operates from a clubhouse that was never fully fitted out when the club moved in. Accumulated deferred maintenance, longstanding OH&S deficiencies, and fragmented storage create an uninviting and operationally difficult environment. The gear shed is the largest single source of OH&S risk: no engineered storage, makeshift solutions in active use, a dual-axle road trailer that must be turned by hand, and floor-level gear that obstructs the emergency access walkway. The function room has an unusable echo, inadequate AV, and insufficient storage. The operational wing has misaligned office configuration, an idle washing machine (four years unplumbed), and a PA microphone broken for years. The club has no funded, coordinated plan to address these issues.

Separately, GS-03 (custom mobile rescue board racks) requires a fabricator-ready drawing pack. A Rev-A addendum was issued after a fabricator review found six blockers in the original drawings (height stack-up failed, frame perimeter ambiguous, roller geometry wrong); the Rev-B final pack is still in design.

## Vision

A single coordinated Clubhouse Renewal Project, funded through the Victorian Emergency Services Equipment Program (VESEP) on a 2:1 government-to-club contribution ratio, that transforms the clubhouse from compromise to fit-for-purpose in one program of works. The club's one-third contribution is drawn from its existing term deposit at maturity and fully repaid from 50% of net functions revenue within six months, leaving cash reserves at their starting level with no impact on operating cash flow.

Alongside the capital program, a complete fabricator-ready drawing pack for the custom mobile board racks (GS-03) is delivered: two identical mobile racks for the NE quadrant of the gear shed, each carrying an IRB via a slide-then-tilt sled mechanism at safe handling height.

The change in a line: from "wow, there is crap everywhere" to "wow, this is an impressive clubhouse."

